.TH SIGNUM 1 "2026" "Signum" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
signum \- P1/P2/P3 HF data modem with the classic host-mode interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B signum
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B signum-start
[\-\-no\-tui] [\-\-bin PATH] [\fIsignum options\fR]
.br
.B signum-loopback
start|stop|status
.SH DESCRIPTION
Signum is an HF data modem (P1, P2 and P3) behind the classic host-mode
serial interface on a pty, with a web GUI, a WebSocket API, a command shell
and a command interpreter on raw TCP (telnet). \fBsignum-start\fR runs it
together with the terminal front panel; \fBsignum-loopback\fR runs two
stations joined by the ALSA loopback for trying it without a radio.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-call " CALL"
Own call sign (default N0CALL).
.TP
.BI \-\-pty " PATH"
Host-mode serial interface (default /tmp/ttysignum0; \fBnone\fR: no pty).
.TP
.BI \-\-alsa " DEV"
Sound card (for example plughw:CARD=CODEC), 48 kHz mono.
.TP
.BI \-\-audio\-in " F " \-\-audio\-out " F"
Lockstep FIFO audio link instead (tests, a second instance).
.TP
.BI \-\-ptt " SPEC"
rts:TTY, dtr:TTY, civ:TTY:ADDR[:BAUD], rig[:HOST[:PORT]], vox or none.
civ: needs the radio's CI-V address in hex (IC-7300: 94); BAUD defaults to 19200.
rig: talks to Hamlib's rigctld, default 127.0.0.1:4532; it also reads the
radio's frequency for the front panel (\fBf\fR, read only, every 2 s in
standby, never during a call or a link).
Do not use vox: the far station answers about 0.14 s after our packet,
inside a VOX hang time, so its answers are lost (a warning at start).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-key " S"
Key-down watchdog (default 90 s).
.TP
.BI \-\-tx\-gain " DB"
Output level (default 0).
.TP
.BI \-\-tones " LO,HI"
Tone pair (default 1400,1600).
.TP
.BI \-\-mylevel " 1|2|3"
Highest level (default 3).
.TP
.BI \-\-pduplex " on|off"
Automatic changeover in every mode (the \fBPDuplex\fR command: 1 or 0).
\fBoff\fR (the default, as the reference unit): while receiving, Signum
breaks in only when the sender hands the link over, when the operator
presses the break-in key (Ctrl-Y; host mode \fB%O\fR or \fB%I\fR) or to
disconnect; text typed while receiving waits. While sending, it hands the
link over only on Ctrl-Y (host mode \fB%O\fR). \fBon\fR: it also breaks in
whenever text is waiting, except within \fB\-\-pdtimer\fR seconds of the
other station's break-in, and hands over whenever its text has run out.
.TP
.BI \-\-pdtimer " S"
The \fBPDTimer\fR command: 2\-30 s, default 12 (used with \fB\-\-pduplex on\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-listen
Listen mode on at start.
.TP
.BI \-\-tune\-tone " S"
Tune for up to S seconds (1\-10; default 0 = off) before each new call
we place, so an automatic antenna tuner can tune: the radio's own tuner cycle
through rigctld, or a steady tone (see \fB\-\-tune\-method\fR); then call as
usual. Not on the call's own repeats, not as callee, not for unproto.
Console command \fBTUnetone\fR [S [HZ [DB]]] [auto|tone|radio].
.TP
.BI \-\-tune\-method " auto|tone|radio"
How to tune. \fBauto\fR (the default): the radio's own tune cycle (rigctld
\fBG TUNE\fR) when \fB\-\-ptt rig\fR reaches a rigctld whose rig lists the TUNE
VFO operation (\fB\\dump_caps\fR, asked once per connection), otherwise the tone.
\fBtone\fR: always the tone. \fBradio\fR: the radio's tune; when that is not
possible the log says why and the tone is sent, never nothing. The radio's
tune transmits at the \fIradio's\fR RF POWER setting, not at the data level;
Signum does not key itself; an abort drops the call. The wait ends when the
radio has keyed and is back in receive, else after S seconds. The log's
\fB[tune]\fR lines say which method was used and why.
.TP
.BI \-\-tune\-hz " HZ"
The tuning tone's frequency, 300\-2700 Hz (default 1500).
.TP
.BI \-\-tune\-db " DB"
The tuning tone's level against the data level, \-30 to 0 dB (default 0:
the same power as the P1 call packets).
.TP
.BI \-\-alc\-warn " on|off"
Warn when the radio's ALC acts while we send P2/P3 (console command
\fBALCwarn\fR 1 or 0). \fBon\fR (the default): while keyed on a P2/P3 link,
when the PTT is \fBrig:\fR, Signum reads the radio's ALC meter through
rigctld (\fBl ALC\fR) every 0.25 s on a connection of its own, and writes one
\fB[alc]\fR warning to the log per link (also on the GUI's Drive (ALC) card
and in the WebSocket panel's \fBalc\fR object) when the ALC reached 0.50 on 3 of
the last 6 bursts, with a summary at the link's end. It only warns: it never
changes the TX gain or the radio's power. Without a rigctld PTT, or when the
radio gives no ALC reading, it says so once and does nothing. \fBoff\fR reads
nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-peer\-marker " on|off"
The Signum peer marker (console command \fBPEERmark\fR 1 or 0). \fBon\fR
(the default, a deliberate difference from the reference unit, which has
none): once on each P2/P3 link Signum sends a short system sequence that
the reference unit skips silently, so that two Signums recognise each other
(the front panel's Peer lamps: Signum, Other). Never sent on P1. \fBoff\fR:
nothing extra is sent.
.TP
.B \-\-p3\-unproto\-rx
Listen mode also prints P3 unproto transmissions (U 30-41). Off by default:
the reference unit has no P3 unproto receiver.
.TP
.BI \-\-prime\-ms " MS"
Playback prime (default 40 ms).
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
No modem log on standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-cal " FILE"
Timing calibration (default ~/.config/signum/calibration.conf).
.TP
.BI \-\-rt\-latency " S", " \-\-audio\-ppm " E
Override the calibration file.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-autocal
No self-calibration (by default the sound card's rate error and the hardware
round trip are measured from links when the file does not give them).
.TP
.BI \-\-autocal\-state " FILE"
Where the self-calibration keeps what it measured (default
~/.config/signum/autocal-DEV.conf, one per sound card).
.TP
.BI \-\-autocal\-max\-ms " N"
Self-calibration: hardware round-trip readings above N ms are refused
(default 45; environment AUTOCAL_HW_MAX_MS=N the same, the option wins).
Raise it for a radio path known to be slower (an SDR transceiver); a far
station on this modem code, which answers some 60 ms later than the
reference unit, is then taken for hardware too.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu\-adaptive " on|off"
Adaptive CPU features at start (the \fBCPUadapt\fR command: 1 or 0).
\fBon\fR (the default): features that need more computing are used and
switched off, level by level, when this computer's decodes come late; for
now one feature, long packets as P3 receiver (a late long decode at level n
stops them above n \- 1, one with less than 8 ms to spare above n; kept from
link to link). \fBoff\fR: never used (no retries on a slow computer). The
environment variable P3_LONG_RT=0 or 1 forces never or always (tests).
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu\-clock " auto|max|off"
With \-\-alsa: hold the CPU clock at its maximum while a link is up
(\fBauto\fR, the default), for the whole run (\fBmax\fR), or never
(\fBoff\fR). Each cpufreq policy's scaling_min_freq is set to its
scaling_max_freq and written back in standby and on exit; it needs that file
writable (example unit signum-cpufreq.service in the examples directory).
.TP
.BI \-\-telnet " PORT"
Command interpreter on TCP (default 3000; 0 = off), with
.BI \-\-telnet\-bind " ADDR"
and
.BI \-\-telnet\-auth " PASS".
.TP
.BI \-\-ws " [ADDR:]PORT"
GUI and WebSocket address (default 127.0.0.1:8770);
.B \-\-no\-ws
turns them off.
.TP
.BI \-\-web\-auth " USER:PASS", " \-\-ws\-auth " USER:PASS
Logins for the GUI pages and for the WebSocket API (open by default);
\fB\-\-web\-auth\-file\fR F and \fB\-\-ws\-auth\-file\fR F read USER:PASS from
the first line of a file that is not a # comment.
.TP
.BI \-\-gui " DIR"
Directory served over HTTP (default: the installed www directory).
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
The settings file (default ~/.config/signum/signum.conf, or
.B SIGNUM_CONFIG
in the environment): "option = value" lines, the long options without
"\-\-", flags on or off. It is read first, so the command line overrides it.
The GUI's Configure tab writes it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
No settings file (SIGNUM_CONFIG=none does the same).
.TP
.BI \-\-encryption " off|verify|on"
off (default). \fBverify\fR: check the other Signum's identity at link start (the same keys
and handshake as encryption), then send the data in clear; no acknowledgement; a link where the
check cannot be made (the reference unit, an older Signum, an untrusted key) is ended, never
continued unverified. \fBon\fR: encrypt links to other Signums. \fBIllegal on the amateur
radio bands\fR (USA: 47 CFR 97.113(a)(4)); NOT compatible with the AES of SCS
modems (the PTC series) or any other modem's; never falls back to clear text: a link that
cannot be encrypted is ended. Needs \-\-encryption\-ack the first time. Only in a build made
with \fB./configure \-\-enable\-encryption\fR (not the default).
.TP
.B \-\-encryption\-ack NOT-AMATEUR|none
The acknowledgement that this station is not on the amateur bands. The first time
it is given it is stored in the settings file (encryption-ack), and
\fB\-\-encryption on\fR then needs no more. \fBnone\fR withdraws it, removes
encryption from the settings file too, and starts with encryption off.
.TP
.BI \-\-encryption\-trust " stored|tofu|verified"
Encryption's first-contact policy. tofu (default): a new station's key is stored unverified at first contact;
stored: only a key already stored; verified: only keys you verified.
.TP
.BI \-\-verify\-trust " stored|tofu|verified"
The same for verify; default stored.
.TP
.BI \-\-sign " on|off"
Verify: an authentication tag on every record (the data still in clear; a bad tag ends the
link). off (default): the identity is checked at link start only, and a takeover after the
check is not detected. No extra effect with encryption on.
.TP
.BI \-\-encryption\-wait " S"
The capability record and the handshake, each at most S seconds (10-600,
default 60).
.TP
.BI \-\-encryption\-compression " on|off"
Compress before encrypting (zstd with a dictionary; default on). Used only
when both Signums have it on; otherwise the records go uncompressed.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-dir " DIR"
The encryption keys (default ~/.config/signum/keys; the environment variable SIGNUM_KEY_DIR replaces the default).
.TP
.B \-\-keygen, \-\-show\-key
Make this station's key pair if it has none, or show it; print the public
key and its fingerprint, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version, \-\-help
.SS Icom IC-7300 set-up
One action each, then Signum exits. None changes the radio's transmit power
or frequency, and none transmits.
.TP
.B \-\-detect\-ic7300
Find every IC-7300 (USB serial port, sound card, CI-V rate and address) and
print its settings and what the set-up would change. Reads only.
.TP
.B \-\-backup\-ic7300\-settings
Save the radio's settings to ~/.config/signum/ic7300-backup-SERIAL-TIME.conf.
Changes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-configure\-my\-ic7300\-and\-overwrite\-its\-settings
RECONFIGURE the radio for Signum over CI-V (Connectors menu, USB-D, 3.0 kHz
filter, AGC MID, NB/NR/notch/COMP/VOX/split/RIT/XIT off, USB SEND = RTS),
each setting read back, after a backup of its settings and your typed YES;
then write alsa, ptt, tx-gain, prime-ms and no-autocal into the settings file.
.TP
.B \-\-configure\-my\-ic7300\-for\-an\-external\-modem\-and\-overwrite\-its\-settings
The same, for a hardware modem on the radio's ACC socket instead of Signum on
USB: DATA MOD = ACC, USB SEND and USB Keying (CW, RTTY) OFF (nothing on USB can
key the radio), the rest as for Signum, so either command brings the radio
back from the other. Does not write the settings file; Signum cannot key the
radio until the Signum set-up is run again.
.TP
.BI \-\-restore\-ic7300\-settings " FILE"
Write such a backup back to its radio, each setting read back, after your
typed YES.
.TP
.BI \-\-ic7300\-serial " N"
Which radio, when more than one is plugged in.
.TP
.B \-\-i\-understand\-this\-overwrites\-my\-ic7300\-settings
For scripts: confirm the set-up or restore without typing YES.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/signum/signum.conf
Settings read at start (see \-\-config); written 0600 by the GUI.
.TP
.I ~/.config/signum/ic7300-backup-SERIAL-TIME.conf
An IC-7300's settings saved by the set-up (or \-\-backup\-ic7300\-settings);
\-\-restore\-ic7300\-settings writes them back.
.TP
.I ~/.config/signum/keys/
Encryption keys: station.key (the private key, 0600), peers, psk, received.
.TP
.I ~/.config/signum/calibration.conf
Timing calibration; a commented sample is in the system configuration
directory (signum/calibration.conf).
.TP
.I share/signum/www, share/signum/tui
The GUI and the terminal front panel.
.SH NOTES
Real-time scheduling needs
.B setcap cap_sys_nice,cap_ipc_lock+ep
on the binary; \fBmake\fR and \fBmake install\fR set it (\fBcap_sys_nice,cap_ipc_lock+ep\fR) when run as root or with password-less sudo, \fBmake rt\fR otherwise.
.PP
\fBCPU health.\fR Signum tells when this computer is not keeping up with the audio and the
decodes (information only; \fBCPUadapt\fR is the one control). The state is
\fBOK\fR, \fBBUSY\fR or \fBSTARVED\fR, from playback underruns and capture
stalls, late or thin decodes, computer back-off steps, SL1 rescues and late
transmit audio in the last minute (start-up hiccups are ignored; it does not
flap). It shows as a lamp on the GUI panel (reason in its tooltip), on the
TUI's top line, as the console command \fBCPULoad\fR, as the \fBcpu\fR field
of the WebSocket panel message, and as a \fB[cpu]\fR log line on every change
that says what is late, which of no real-time priority, a CPU clock that
cannot be held up and other load apply here, and what to do: stop other
programs, \fBmake rt\fR, the signum-cpufreq.service unit, or a faster
computer. Only with \-\-alsa.
